# Build context exclusions for infra/agentcore/Dockerfile.
#
# This file sits at the REPOSITORY ROOT on purpose. A .dockerignore is read from
# the build CONTEXT root, not from the directory holding the Dockerfile, and the
# context for this image must be the repo root (the Dockerfile copies
# pyproject.toml, agentorg/ and fixtures/). A copy under infra/agentcore/ would
# be silently ignored -- which is the failure mode this comment exists to prevent.
#
# Build with:
#   docker build -f infra/agentcore/Dockerfile .

# THE IMPORTANT ONE. build/lib/agentorg/ holds a STALE copy of several modules
# from before the packaging fix, missing the agents/, common/ and security/
# subpackages. It is gitignored (.gitignore:9) but present in the worktree, so a
# build context would carry it. Nothing may read it as truth.
build/
dist/
*.egg-info/

# Never ship a virtualenv into an image. .venv-sorour holds an EDITABLE install
# whose .pth file redirects `agentorg` imports back at this worktree -- inside a
# container that path does not exist, and if it did it would defeat the
# non-editable install the image is built to exercise.
.venv/
.venv-*/
venv/

# Secrets. .env holds a live GitHub token (gitignored). Excluded here as well so
# no build context can carry it into an image layer, where it would persist even
# if a later stage deleted it.
.env
*.tfvars

# Gitignored scratch that grows on every test run; pure noise in an image.
runs/
runs-archive-*/

# Caches: stale bytecode has served wrong bytes in this repo before, and a host
# __pycache__ can shadow freshly installed source.
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
.pytest_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# Not needed at runtime. The agents are library code; the suite, the demo target
# app, the Terraform and the planning docs are all build-time or human artifacts.
tests/
target_repo/
docs/
tickets/
scripts/
infra/Terraform/
.github/
.superpowers/
make_fixtures.py

# Git metadata: large, and it would put the whole history in a layer.
.git/
.gitignore
